{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1484292-thoughts-on-population","id":1484292,"title":"Thoughts on population","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-06-17T10:48:51-07:00","id":1484292,"title":"Thoughts on population","content":"A lot of people will probably disagree with me, but I think the WTA setting works better if the largest population of Garou(and by extent other fera) are Lupus-born.<br /> <br /> Setting-wise, the garou social set-up doesn't make sense to me if the largest group of garou are initially human-raised before the first change. Homids are just going to bring their own cultures and ideas into the garou nation because that's what they grew up with and they aren't going to hold as closely to garou tradition because they didn't grow up with that. Lupus though? The first experience Lupus will have with full sapience will Be their initiation into the garou nation. it'll be the first things they're taught and the things they'll compare everything else to. Unlike homids, Lupus are More likely to hold onto garou traditions the way the nation as a whole is painted as doing in the lore.<br /> <br /> It also works much better mathmatically as well. Even going by the rule that says garou only have a 10%+ chance(depending on pure breed) of being born each breeding cycle and only 1 pup will be born garou, Wolves still produce litters of Kinfolk, all of who have a 1%+ chance(depending on pure breed) of producing garou after only growing up for 2 years. Kin populations also have the &quot;evolutionary&quot; advantage of being watched over by garou, so they have a much higher chance of survival than normal wolves. Finally, Lupus Garou themselves have an exponentially longer lifespan compared to normal wolves, and wolves are known to move on to choose other mates when one dies. Statistically speaking, Lupus have a much higher chance of producing garou.<br /> <br /> No matter what angle I look at this, the massive canon Homid population just seems to contradict the logic of the setting itself.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-17T11:26:17-07:00","id":1484294,"title":"","content":"The setting as written is that. But I recommend looking at the various NPCs and templates in the books because they tell a different story.<br /> <br /> A massive issue with WtA's writing, I think, is that the Nation is written as this Luddite feral culture that rarely if ever tolerates modern inventions and sticks to their traditions. But look at any sourcebook and you will find NPCs of non-Gnawer non-Glass Walker tribes with solid human ties and generally written as present in the modern world. I think only Younger Brother revised had NPCs actually avoid modernity.<br /> <br /> So yes, you are correct that the setting as written in the corebooks does not really match the setting depicted in any sourcebook.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-17T12:20:25-07:00","id":1484296,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1484292-thoughts-on-population#post1484292\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A lot of people will probably disagree with me, but I think the WTA setting works better if the largest population of Garou(and by extent other fera) are Lupus-born.</div> </div> </div> </div>The modern Garou nation being dominated by homids is somewhat inevitable though. There's 8 billion humans on Earth, compared to 1/4 million wild <i>Canis lupus</i> and if you want to really skew things in the favor of the lupus between things like wild African dogs being viable wolf-kin, let's say there's 1/2 million potential &quot;wolves&quot; to breed with. That's ~16K humans for every &quot;wolf.&quot; There's no way for the lupus to be the largest population without a huge amount of handwavium going on.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Setting-wise, the garou social set-up doesn't make sense to me if the largest group of garou are initially human-raised before the first change. Homids are just going to bring their own cultures and ideas into the garou nation because that's what they grew up with and they aren't going to hold as closely to garou tradition because they didn't grow up with that. </div> </div> </div>I... don't really buy this assumption. The Tribes are full of human cultural assumptions, which then filter into Sept and Garou Nation culture. How would there even be Tribes if the lupus were running things? The Garou Nation is, as written, even with many of the inconsistencies Ana Mizuki mentioned, as a very human skewed culture. Homids might have some culture shock (or not, not all homids are Lost Cubs and the increase in Lost Cubs is supposed to be recent in the setting, meaning that for most of its history the Garou Nation has been raising homids in communities controlled by the Garou (at least via kinfolk) to ease transition. The Glass Walkers, who have the most over difficulty here, have a well practiced indoctrination method that takes advantage of the first few days of shock after a homid's First Change to get past this hurdle ASAP.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Lupus though? The first experience Lupus will have with full sapience will Be their initiation into the garou nation. it'll be the first things they're taught and the things they'll compare everything else to. Unlike homids, Lupus are More likely to hold onto garou traditions the way the nation as a whole is painted as doing in the lore. </div> </div> </div>I also don't think that really plays out. Culture shock is certainly a thing, but &quot;sapience shock&quot; is something to take into consideration as well. Garou raised in human cultures might struggle to adjust to Garou culture, but they know what a culture is, they understand norms and unspoken complex social dynamics, and overlapping political interactions. The sudden onset of human level sapience for the lupus doesn't mean they're going to instantly understand or desire these things. Garou cultural hierarchy and norms are something they have zero experience with to try to appreciate what they're being taught. Rejection to such teachings is a serious risk However much I don't like the Red Talons... they tend to have a point about how the Garou Nation is nothing like how a lupus dominated organization would look like, and have some pretty strong disgust for certain rules that they're forced to abide by. <br /> <br /> Whatever issues the Garou Nation has as a homid-focused institution, it has even more to be what it is if it were a lupus-oriented one.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Lupus have a much higher chance of producing garou. </div> </div> </div>Per capita yes, but not enough to come close to the 16,000:1 ratio of potential breeding partners out there.<br /> <br /> If the Garou Nation is already &quot;only&quot; 70% homid vs. 20% lupus, that's already not really in line with the math as it is in favor of having significant lupus populations to be viable PCs. Do you really think all the per capita advantages you're bringing up can really account for 16,000:1 being reduced to 2.5:1, let alone 1:1 or even going in the lupus's favor?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-17T13:26:26-07:00","id":1484297,"title":"","content":"At this point, you aren't doing a werewolf game at all, but some fantasy world completely divorced from the myths and legends that the Gothic tradition is based on. Legends and folktales are clear - werewolves are humans who turn into wolves. Wolves who act like people are more like fairy tale figures. My personal preference is that having Lupus and Metis was a design mistake in the original game, but the setting is what it is. I live with it.<br /> <br /> I don't find the culture issue to be much of an issue. Many kinfolk societies are probably fairly isolated from mainstream humanity, and are based much more on Garou culture and values. They can prepare their children adequately well. And since most Garou are supposed to experience their first change in early puberty, that is more than enough time to feed them propaganda. Sure there is an increasing number of Lost Cubs, late changing Garou who are already adults, and kinfolk groups having to compromise their culture because they are more integrated with humanity. The Garou that come out of that will be oddballs who may not fit in.<br /> <br /> Does the setting detailed in Rage books and other sourcebooks actually portray that well? I agree with Ana Mizuki that it doesn't. The writers often ignore important elements of the game setting like the Curse. This is just another. Players often create PCs that are adult Lost Cubs instead of teenagers who changed at 13. But if this is important to you, it isn't too hard to fix for STs creating their own settings, or for Players wanting to create PCs that should be more typical.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1484292-thoughts-on-population#post1484292\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It also works much better mathmatically as well. Even going by the rule that says garou only have a 10%+ chance(depending on pure breed) of being born each breeding cycle and only 1 pup will be born garou, Wolves still produce litters of Kinfolk, all of who have a 1%+ chance(depending on pure breed) of producing garou after only growing up for 2 years.</div> </div> </div> </div>Since the game setting clearly does not reflect this, using that mathematically percentage must be clearly wrong when it comes to Lupus. Since Lupus Garou clearly cannot repopulate that easily, there must be some other percentage more appropriate for them.<br /> <br /> The percentage probably doesn't even work well for homids to be honest.<br /> <br /> It's obvious that the 1 in 10 and 1 in 100 chance is an artifact of the game using d10s. The people who wrote that (I forgot which book, the original Kinfolk one or was it some Players Guide?) probably didn't try to figure out a realistic chance that a child would be born Garou. They didn't create an actual population of kinfolk and Garou, mortality rates, fertility rates, and all the other demographic information necessary to figure out a Garou birth rate that would be near stable, but slightly declining so that over the years less Garou would be noticeably born and the total population declining.<br /> <br /> Instead it was something provided so that STs could quickly and randomly determine whether a child would be Garou for PCs and NPCs in his chronicle, and then move on. It's a quick and dirty tool for an ST so he can determine something and quickly move on. It's not some magical formula that drives the game setting. It's good enough to do what it was intended for. People should stop treating it like it's a scientific analysis for Garou demographics. That's my humble suggestion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 06-17-2022, 04:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-17T18:57:15-07:00","id":1484320,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1484292-thoughts-on-population?p=1484296#post1484296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The modern Garou nation being dominated by homids is somewhat inevitable though. There's 8 billion humans on Earth, compared to 1/4 million wild <i>Canis lupus</i> and if you want to really skew things in the favor of the lupus between things like wild African dogs being viable wolf-kin, let's say there's 1/2 million potential &quot;wolves&quot; to breed with. That's ~16K humans for every &quot;wolf.&quot; There's no way for the lupus to be the largest population without a huge amount of handwavium going on.</div> </div> </div> </div>If you go over the math of how fast wolves breed, it's much less hand wavium.<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>You have 16,000:1 human-to-wolf ratio,</li> <li>wolves reach adulthood at 2, humans at 18(some teenagers are dumb and accidentally have kids at 14-16, but that's not the norm).</li> <li>Each homid and lupus garou has a 10% chance(ignoring pure-breed) of producing garou offspring with kin</li> <li>Garoun/non-kin offspring are always kin, Kin/Kin offspring produce garou 1% of the time, Kin 50% of the time, and non-kin the rest(ignoring pure breed).</li> <li>Humans have 1-3 offspring per paring(average of 2.4 in 2019), wolves produce 2-6(no number average so I'll say 5)</li> </ol>Doing the math, in ideal garou conditions for both sides: Mulitply both human and wolf sides of the ration by 300 for our initial sample(4.8 million humans, 300 wolves). Assuming a conservative estimate of 1:400,000 garou to human ratio(roughly 14), basically only enough to be a rounding error for humans and not enough to give a fair ratio to both sides.<br /> <br /> Humans: 2.4 million couples, producing 2.4 children per couple(over Lifetime), a generation span of 18 years, a 1% chance of Garou and 50% chance of kin. <b>After 18 years:</b> 1st generation produces 2.88 million kin, 24,000 werewolves.<br /> <br /> Wolves: 150 couples, Producing 5 children per Year, a generation span of 2 years, 1% garou, and 50% kin. <b>Year 1:</b> 375 kin and 1 garou produced(gen 2A). Total: 525 Kin and 1 garou, <b>Year 2:</b> 375 Kin and 1 Garou produced again(gen 2b). Total: 900 Kin and 2 garou. <b>Year 3</b>(gen 2A reaches maturity): 1315 kin produced and 5 garou produced(gen 3[technically 3a+ 2c, but that is going to get way too complicated]) Total: 2210 kin and 7 garou <b>Year 4</b>(generation 2b reaches maturity): 2255 kin produced and 9 Garou produced(gen 4). Total: 4465 kin + 16 Garou. <b>Year 5</b>(gen 3 reach maturity, gen 1 assumed dies and does not contribute): 5400 kin produced and 20 garou produced(gen 5). Total: 9715 kin and 36 garou. <b>Year 6</b>(Gen 4 reaches maturity): 10785 kin produced and 44 Garou produced(gen 6). Total: 20500 kin and 80 garou. <b>Year 7</b>(generation 5 matures, generation 2a is assumed to have died): 24,100 kin and 97 garou produced(gen 7) Total: 44225 kin and 177 garou. <b>Year 8</b>(generation 6 matures, gen 2b dies): 50,312 kin and 205 garou produced.(gen 8) Total: 94537 kin and 382 garou. <b>Year 9</b>(gen 7 matures, gen 3 dies): 56712 kin and 442 garou produced(gen 9). Total: 151249 kin and 824 garou. <b>Year 10</b>(generation 8 matures, gen 4 dies): 235780 kin and 943 garou produced(gen 10) Total: 387029 kin and 1767 Garou. <b>Year 11</b>(gen 9 matures, gen 5 dies): 364622 kin and 1458 garou produced(gen 11) Total: 510471 kin and 3225. <b>Year 12</b>(gen 10 matures, gen 6 dies): 940610 kin and 3762 garou produced(gen 12) Total: 1440296 kin and 6987 garou. <b>Year 13</b>(gen 11 matures, gen 7 dies): 1215927 kin and 4863 garou produce(gen 13) Total: 2632623 kin and 11850 garou(gen 13) <b>Year 14</b>(gen 12 matures, gen 8 dies): 3474960 kin and 13899 garou Total: 6,107,503 kin and 25749 Garou. I'm ending here because <i>Screw Math.</i><br /> <br /> Why are the above wolf numbers so large? Because wolf kin on average effectively produce 2.5X their population in Pup-kin <b>Every Year</b> while human kinfold produce an average of 1.2x their population <b>Over their entire Lifetime.</b> Considering that I used a Very conservative estimate of a 6 year lifespan for the wolf kinfolk, the wolf kinfold will produce 10x their population over their lifetime in kin.<br /> <br /> So why are wild wolves dying in the first place? Short answer: because the garou are idiots. They have a Large number of advantages that can offset this entirely, not the least of which is their ability to use rites open up pocket-dimensions with abundant resources to house wolves away from the encroachment of civilization.<br /> <br /> Granted my argument was never that there should be more wild wolves than people. My argument was that the 16,000:1 Human kinfolk to wolf kinfolk figure would have <b>Never Reached That Point</b>. The way wolves and humans multiply should result in an exponentially higher percentage of Wolves being born as garou-kin than humans being born garou kin relative to their non-kin populations. That also adds to the tragedy of the wolf population dying out, because if werewolves are more dependent on the wolf population than the human population to increase their numbers, less wolves = Drastically less garou<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1484292-thoughts-on-population?p=1484297#post1484297\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">At this point, you aren't doing a werewolf game at all, but some fantasy world completely divorced from the myths and legends that the Gothic tradition is based on. Legends and folktales are clear - werewolves are humans who turn into wolves. Wolves who act like people are more like fairy tale figures. My personal preference is that having Lupus and Metis was a design mistake in the original game, but the setting is what it is. I live with it.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I disagree? I feel like the WtA setting would be exactly the same if you had the majority of Garou be lupus-born, and that's my central point.<br /> <br /> WoD has already gone Far away from the classic fairy tale and gothic tradition. The WoD werewolves are shamanic spirit wizards who fight a theological war against captain planet villains, nature-cthulhu, and Evil shamanic spirit wizards. The only things it has in common with traditional werewolf lore is that there Are werewolves.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1484292-thoughts-on-population?p=1484297#post1484297\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Since the game setting clearly does not reflect this, using that mathematically percentage must be clearly wrong when it comes to Lupus. Since Lupus Garou clearly cannot repopulate that easily, there must be some other percentage more appropriate for them.</div> </div> </div> </div>So alter the lore to reflect the setting rather than altering the setting to reflect the lore? Ultimately a change more up to flavor considering both changes are trying to accomplish the same thing: Fix a discrepancy between the setting and the lore without fundumentally altering the setting into anything unrecognizable","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-17T19:41:22-07:00","id":1484325,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1484292-thoughts-on-population?p=1484320#post1484320\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why are the above wolf numbers so large?</div> </div> </div> </div>Mostly because you seem to be assuming some sort of highly managed wolf breeding program where Garou deny their wolf kin the ability to live as wild wolfs in natural packs (which explicitly leads to them no longer being kin over a few generations). Wolves are capable of breeding in such numbers,but that's not how wild wolves actually work. The vast majority of wolves never reproduce because there's generally only one breeding pair per pack. The other female wolves in the pack will enter false pregnancies due to hormonal responses to a pregnancy in the pack, so you can't even have a Garou sneak in and knock up more females than average (which would likely destroy the pack anyway).<br /> <br /> This is also ignoring that average human reproduction is largely meaningless towards what happens in relatively small purposefully isolated communities (aka what your average human kin groups are going to be like while you're massively knocking down the number of humans in your math) where they're likely to have much larger than average families. Your average Amish family, as an example, has 7 children per couple, not 2.5, and have children younger on average as well (since they're less likely to pursue higher education and middle/upper middle class work that delays birthing years in most wealthy countries).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They have a Large number of advantages that can offset this entirely, not the least of which is their ability to use rites open up pocket-dimensions with abundant resources to house wolves away from the encroachment of civilization. </div> </div> </div>The books generally do a good job of making this harder than it could be in order to maintain the setting as intended. Putting wolves into managed breeding programs &quot;tames them&quot; and then like dogs they lose their spiritual connection to breeding with the Garou. Putting wolves in the Umbra turns them into spirits instead of natural wolves. Etc.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> My argument was that the 16,000:1 Human kinfolk to wolf kinfolk figure would have <b>Never Reached That Point</b>. </div> </div> </div>You're asserting this with very little reasons to accept it as a given.<br /> <br /> Unless you're going to say the Garou have <b>massive</b> secret populations of wolves in their original ranges, the explosion of humanity was going to force wolves into too small of a territory to maintain the kind of numbers you'd need them too for what you're talking about. The vast majority of humans live in a place with no remaining wild wolves, or never had any in the first place. But the Garou still need to do their duties in those places. Once the Garou stopped culling humanity, this outcome was inevitable because of how humans spread (which is more than just birth rates).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That also adds to the tragedy of the wolf population dying out, because if werewolves are more dependent on the wolf population than the human population to increase their numbers, less wolves = Drastically less garou </div> </div> </div>This is already in the books.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I disagree? I feel like the WtA setting would be exactly the same if you had the majority of Garou be lupus-born, and that's my central point. </div> </div> </div>But you really need to explain how this is the case. I brought up a bunch of stuff in my post you skipped to focus on math. How would the Fianna exist virtually the same as they do now if the majority of Garou were lupus? How would the Black Furies ever gotten big enough to be recognized as a Tribe with such as strong focus on a human problem?<br /> <br /> Why would lupus be more inclined towards Garou indoctrination when they're being told to value concepts they have no life experience with instead of shifting norms based on a major life changing event?<br /> <br /> Saying the setting would be exactly the same clearly isn't as convincing as you seem to feel it is.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The only things it has in common with traditional werewolf lore is that there Are werewolves. </div> </div> </div>More importantly... there isn't really any &quot;traditional&quot; werewolf that happened like some of the other supernaturals critters the WoD uses. When Bram Stoker just mashed dozens of mythological stories together into Dracula that... stuck and lots of people copied him. Werewolves never really had that happen (a lot of things in WtA are actually in various traditional mythologies, but there's no majorly successful Gothic literary work that decided which of those would dominate mainstream European-based pop-culture).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-17T21:08:52-07:00","id":1484327,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1484292-thoughts-on-population?p=1484325#post1484325\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Mostly because you seem to be assuming some sort of highly managed wolf breeding program where Garou deny their wolf kin the ability to live as wild wolfs in natural packs (<b>which explicitly leads to them no longer being kin over a few generations</b>). Wolves are capable of breeding in such numbers,but that's not how wild wolves actually work. The vast majority of wolves never reproduce because there's generally only one breeding pair per pack. The other female wolves in the pack will enter false pregnancies due to hormonal responses to a pregnancy in the pack, so you can't even have a Garou sneak in and knock up more females than average (which would likely destroy the pack anyway).</div> </div> </div> </div>Doesn't that only happen when humans <i>Domesticate</i> kin? Domestication is a very specific breeding process that purposefully out-breeds the normal wild instincts of the animal, which would be the exact opposite of what the garou would be doing. Besides that, opening up spirit groves and pocket realms for the wolves to flourish in disconnected from the ravaged lands of earth would be preferable to that.<br /> <br /> As for how wild wolves breed. That's not really how wolves work either. Wolf pack are generally a breeding pair and their Offspring who are sticking around to mature before breaking off from the pack to mate and build their own. The reason most wolves don't normally end up reproducing is because they die, not because they're hormonally prevented from doing so, the hormonal responses you described are mechanisms for preventing incest in their parent pack. The main breeding pair also Do reproduce on yearly cycles as long as there's sufficient food.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1484292-thoughts-on-population?p=1484325#post1484325\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is also ignoring that average human reproduction is largely meaningless towards what happens in relatively small purposefully isolated communities (aka what your average human kin groups are going to be like while you're massively knocking down the number of humans in your math) where they're likely to have much larger than average families. Your average Amish family, as an example, has 7 children per couple, not 2.5, and have children younger on average as well (since they're less likely to pursue higher education and middle/upper middle class work that delays birthing years in most wealthy countries).</div> </div> </div> </div>That's still 7 children per couple, in remote areas, vs Every wolf pair producing 15-20 pups over their lifetime(most of which Die IRL, but they don't have Garou uncles).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1484292-thoughts-on-population?p=1484325#post1484325\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Unless you're going to say the Garou have <b>massive</b> secret populations of wolves in their original ranges,</div> </div> </div> </div>you're missing my point entirely. I'm not saying there are more wolves in the theoretical setting I'm presenting, I'm saying there are more ratio kin wolves to non-kin wolves vs the ratio of kin humans to non-kin humans. Something like 50-100% of wild wolves as were-kin vs a very low percentage of human kinfolk-to-normal humans thanks to the longevity of garou verses wolves and number of wolves born per cycle.<br /> <br /> This makes the 16000:1 figure entirely meaningless, as very few of those 16,000 humans would actually be kin.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1484292-thoughts-on-population?p=1484325#post1484325\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is already in the books.</div> </div> </div> </div>I know, but having the homids be rarer highlights this better<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1484292-thoughts-on-population?p=1484325#post1484325\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But you really need to explain how this is the case. I brought up a bunch of stuff in my post you skipped to focus on math. How would the Fianna exist virtually the same as they do now if the majority of Garou were lupus? How would the Black Furies ever gotten big enough to be recognized as a Tribe with such as strong focus on a human problem?<br /> <br /> Why would lupus be more inclined towards Garou indoctrination when they're being told to value concepts they have no life experience with instead of shifting norms based on a major life changing event?<br /> <br /> Saying the setting would be exactly the same clearly isn't as convincing as you seem to feel it is.</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay, these are good points I'll have to think about. I'll definitely need to make some adjustments.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1484292-thoughts-on-population?p=1484325#post1484325\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">More importantly... there isn't really any &quot;traditional&quot; werewolf that happened like some of the other supernaturals critters the WoD uses. When Bram Stoker just mashed dozens of mythological stories together into Dracula that... stuck and lots of people copied him. Werewolves never really had that happen (a lot of things in WtA are actually in various traditional mythologies, but there's no majorly successful Gothic literary work that decided which of those would dominate mainstream European-based pop-culture).</div> </div> </div> </div>I would argue that werewolves did end up with a &quot;traditional&quot; version, but it was created by american hollywood rather than a european source.<br /> <br /> Hollywood is what invented the things that codify the modern werewolf like the silver weakness, the wolf-man hybrid form, and the full moon changing.<br /> <br /> Actual mythology before then had werewolves as actual wolves, the things that slew them were Holy symbols not silver, changes were either yearly+ rituals, permanent curses, nightly or at will(tied to an item), and The way people did change was also different as it'd require a magic ritual, a special magic item, or projecting a separate form while the main body slept.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-17T23:43:02-07:00","id":1484331,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1484292-thoughts-on-population?p=1484297#post1484297\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">At this point, you aren't doing a werewolf game at all, but some fantasy world completely divorced from the myths and legends that the Gothic tradition is based on. Legends and folktales are clear - werewolves are humans who turn into wolves. Wolves who act like people are more like fairy tale figures. My personal preference is that having Lupus and Metis was a design mistake in the original game, but the setting is what it is. I live with it.</div> </div> </div> </div>The myths and legends talk about humans who turn into feral wolf-monsters during full moons and that may infect others with their lycantrophy with their bite, if they survive.<br /> <br /> Magic monsters that turn into 4 different kinds of monster-wolves any time they will, live like noble savages shamans communing with the elements and fighting against basically Cthulhu-esque forces, already have ZERO to do with anything Werewolf related to the legends.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24689-kakost","name":"Kakost","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"586"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-18T00:04:06-07:00","id":1484332,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1484292-thoughts-on-population?p=1484325#post1484325\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is also ignoring that average human reproduction is largely meaningless towards what happens in relatively small purposefully isolated communities (aka what your average human kin groups are going to be like...)</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, the &quot;breeding prisons&quot; of the Garou are going to be exactly what you're describing: isolated communities.<br /> <br /> Which means that your number of &quot;16.000 to 1&quot; humans to wolves ratio is absolutely meaningless, since the Garou will NOT be breeding with 7-8 billions of human beings, but rather with their second cousins at the &quot;breeding farm&quot;. So, I'd say that potential human mates are possibly even smaller than wolf mates.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24689-kakost","name":"Kakost","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"586"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-18T00:15:42-07:00","id":1484333,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1484292-thoughts-on-population?p=1484320#post1484320\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So alter the lore to reflect the setting rather than altering the setting to reflect the lore?</div> </div> </div> </div>The lore and the setting are the same. You are talking about a game mechanic.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-18T00:19:31-07:00","id":1484334,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1484292-thoughts-on-population?p=1484327#post1484327\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would argue that werewolves did end up with a &amp;quot;traditional&amp;quot; version, but it was created by american hollywood rather than a european source.<br /> <br /> Hollywood is what invented the things that codify the modern werewolf like the silver weakness, the wolf-man hybrid form, and the full moon changing.<br /> <br /> Actual mythology before then had werewolves as actual wolves, the things that slew them were Holy symbols not silver, changes were either yearly+ rituals, permanent curses, nightly or at will(tied to an item), and The way people did change was also different as it'd require a magic ritual, a special magic item, or projecting a separate form while the main body slept.</div> </div> </div> </div>There's also the &quot;sixth son of a sixth son&quot; or the &quot;sixth daughter of a sixth daughter&quot; curse, or the infectious bite.<br /> <br /> Anyway, not a single one of the legends has Werewolves as naturalistic magic monsters fighting against the Cthulhu-legion to protect mother nature. The entirety of WtA have ZERO in common to any legend, except that the Garou turn into wolves.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24689-kakost","name":"Kakost","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"586"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-18T00:28:08-07:00","id":1484336,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kakost</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1484292-thoughts-on-population?p=1484331#post1484331\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The myths and legends talk about humans who turn into feral wolf-monsters during full moons and that may infect others with their lycantrophy with their bite, if they survive.</div> </div> </div> </div>That isn't actual folklore and legend. It's Hollywood stuff. That's fine. Hollywood stuff is what many people &quot;know&quot; about werewolves. There's nothing wrong with that. A lot of vampire lore is Hollywood stuff as well. People know it so they expect it. It's just not the entirety.<br /> <br /> But in all cases it is HUMANS turning into wolves. Not wolves turning into humans, which is something I think first showed up in D&amp;D when they added wolfweres in the Monster Manual II. Unless you count fairy tale stories of anthropomorphized wolves.<br /> <br /> The idea of werewolves as shamanic style spirit wizards is not actually that far from the werewolf of legend. Look up actual historical werewolf trials like Thiess of Kaltenbrun. There's a lot of matches. While the game after Revised tends to deliberately downplay connections between the game and actual werewolf lore, that was not the case earlier (see how the concept of Lunatics was introduced in 1e but abandoned and not mentioned in Revised). It's not that hard for an ST to make sufficient connections between the game as is and what many people expect. I've made a lot of posts on this forum showcasing that. A game where &quot;werewolves&quot; are lupus who can turn into humans is nothing like folklore and legend. It's more like the anime Wolf's Rain. Which is OK to a point, as Wolf's Rain hits a lot of WtA themes. One can accommodate that. You're just losing 90% of the game if you only do that.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-18T00:40:10-07:00","id":1484337,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kakost</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1484292-thoughts-on-population?p=1484334#post1484334\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Anyway, not a single one of the legends has Werewolves as naturalistic magic monsters fighting against the Cthulhu-legion to protect mother nature.</div> </div> </div> </div>The historical werewolf trial of Thiess of Kaltenbrunn has the accused, who admits he is a werewolf, describing how werewolves are actually good guys because they enter the spirit world to fight Satan so that people's crops can grow. I think that is close enough.<br /> <br /> Werewolves have often been depicted as sympathetic monsters who fight the other monsters and therefore defend people who would be menaced by them. Every Lon Chaney, Jr. movie after the first one follows that plot. The wolfman fights the Frankenstein monster, mad scientists, and Dracula.<br /> <br /> Now that certainly isn't the only theme. It's just one of many. And whether intentionally or not, the game mirrors that.<br /> <br /> Traditionally, werewolves and other werecreatures (tigers, hyenas, whatever) are evil sorcerers that use magic given to them by (evil) spirits so they can turn into the animals. That's close enough to shamanism for me. It isn't a curse. It's not an infection or disease.<br /> <br /> But in none of traditional lore - whether Hollywood, myth, legend, folktales, or anything else is it the ANIMAL who turns into a human.<br /> <br /> That's fine if you like that. Harkon Lukas in Ravenloft is a great character. It can add things to the game. But what you get is something much farther from real world lore than what the game actually presents us.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-18T06:39:46-07:00","id":1484351,"title":"","content":"I just want to note, the existence of lupus and crinos-born are the reason I've liked WtA so much over the years. I started RP among the various Wolf RPGs of my generation and I found WtA by accident. The ability to not play a human-born has been fun for me, as I struggle being human irl enough already. The animal mind is easier to get into and through it my own weird behaviours don't shine through as much.<br /> <br /> As to the nature of WtA compared to the werewolf folklore, I'd say WtA is still far from it. I have read various folklore books for WtA (thank you Project Guttenberg) and while there are shamanistic aspects to the werewolves and many willing shifters, what the garou are is still very much their own thing distinct from folklore.<br /> <br /> For one, you are born a garou and the only method of turning into one as an adult is HEAVILY frowned upon. You do not perform a ritual, join a specific coven or pledge yourself to go to Hell to fight evil every night. You are born one with no additional sacrifice required.<br /> <br /> Second, the furry blender thing. Yes, folklore werewolves are strong and can regenerate. But the crinos form is something else entirely. The typical folklore werewolf is about as good as glabro in its stats, stronger than humans but not freakishly so. Crinos is so strong that even the most feeble human has the maximum amount of strength a human can have in it. Crinos is clearly made to deal with much MUCH stronger foes than humans.<br /> <br /> Third, garou are not contrasted heavily with humans. Garou are mostly focused on the nature vs civilization issue, not as much how they relate to humans. In fact one of the common ways a werewolf interacts with humans (eating them, using them as a way to turn into a werewolf,etc.) is removed entirely aside from Red Talons or camps who are in the borderlines. Garou are contrasted with each other and the Wyrm, with humans being very much a secondary foe.<br /> <br /> I consider the garou to be their own thing, as a regular werewolf would be vastly different from them.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-18T07:20:50-07:00","id":1484353,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1484292-thoughts-on-population?p=1484336#post1484336\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But in all cases it is HUMANS turning into wolves. Not wolves turning into humans, which is something I think first showed up in D&amp;D when they added wolfweres in the Monster Manual II. Unless you count fairy tale stories of anthropomorphized wolves.</div> </div> </div> </div>I mean, Why wouldn't you? Werewolves in mythology are literally just people who turn into wolves(or other animals like the south american were-llama), they're literally just the reverse version of anthropomorphized animal myths. Making them both the same class of supernatural isn't a big leap in logic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1484292-thoughts-on-population?p=1484336#post1484336\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But in none of traditional lore - whether Hollywood, myth, legend, folktales, or anything else is it the ANIMAL who turns into a human.<br /> <br /> That's fine if you like that. Harkon Lukas in Ravenloft is a great character. It can add things to the game. But what you get is something much farther from real world lore than what the game actually presents us.</div> </div> </div> </div>Anthropomorphized animals are also a staple of fairy tales and folklore that dates back to be fore recorded history, so it'd actually be easy to make the argument that animals turning into people are the &quot;traditional&quot; werewolf with rare exceptions that do the reverse.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1484292-thoughts-on-population?p=1484336#post1484336\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The idea of werewolves as shamanic style spirit wizards is not actually that far from the werewolf of legend. Look up actual historical werewolf trials like Thiess of Kaltenbrun. There's a lot of matches. While the game after Revised tends to deliberately downplay connections between the game and actual werewolf lore, that was not the case earlier (see how the concept of Lunatics was introduced in 1e but abandoned and not mentioned in Revised). It's not that hard for an ST to make sufficient connections between the game as is and what many people expect. I've made a lot of posts on this forum showcasing that. A game where &quot;werewolves&quot; are lupus who can turn into humans is nothing like folklore and legend. It's more like the anime Wolf's Rain. Which is OK to a point, as Wolf's Rain hits a lot of WtA themes. One can accommodate that. You're just losing 90% of the game if you only do that.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1484292-thoughts-on-population?p=1484336#post1484336\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The historical werewolf trial of Thiess of Kaltenbrunn has the accused, who admits he is a werewolf, describing how werewolves are actually good guys because they enter the spirit world to fight Satan so that people's crops can grow. I think that is close enough.<br /> <br /> Werewolves have often been depicted as sympathetic monsters who fight the other monsters and therefore defend people who would be menaced by them. Every Lon Chaney, Jr. movie after the first one follows that plot. The wolfman fights the Frankenstein monster, mad scientists, and Dracula.<br /> <br /> Now that certainly isn't the only theme. It's just one of many. And whether intentionally or not, the game mirrors that.<br /> <br /> Traditionally, werewolves and other werecreatures (tigers, hyenas, whatever) are evil sorcerers that use magic given to them by (evil) spirits so they can turn into the animals. That's close enough to shamanism for me. It isn't a curse. It's not an infection or disease.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Those were actually inventions by the catholic church to reconcile the existence of werewolves in folklore(which people 100% believed at the time) with the then popular idea that thinking pagan magic actually worked was heresy. The writer who invented to concept of &quot;dream wolves&quot; was actually Bishop Augustine of Hippo in his book &quot;The City of God&quot;, who thought that werewolves were actually a collective delusion, where one person would turn into a wolf in their dreams or in a waking hallucination and would hallucinate <i>so Hard </i>that they'd make other people hallucinate them in their imaginary wolf form.<br /> <br /> Later interpretations of pagan rituals became the fear that they were in league with the devil and had to be stopped thanks to the Malleus Maleficarum by Heimrich Kramer(a man who would make Judge Claud Frollo from The Hunchback of Notre Dam look <i>Sane and Well Adjusted</i>) kicked of the ideas that turned into the Witch Hunts in europe. It was the response to the witch hunts and the resulting demonization of previous werewolf myths that spawned the benandanti, the &quot;Good werewolves&quot; and Warriors of the <i>Christian God</i> who fought witches, protected harvests, and dove into hell to retrieve items or people stolen by demons.<br /> <br /> Before then, Werewolves where either people cursed by a god or a bloodline of people with a magic power like the &quot;Werewolves of Ossory&quot; who were descendants of Laighnech Faeladh.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}